From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] sysPrxForUser: sys_lwcond_wait blocks until signalled

Upstream: not yet submitted
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0004)

What: the context-style sys_lwcond in runtime/ppu/ppu_sysprx.cpp gets a real
waiter queue. sys_lwcond_wait queues itself, releases the lwmutex, blocks
until a signal or its timeout (microseconds, 0 forever) and takes the
lwmutex back. sys_lwcond_signal wakes the next waiter by the paired
lwmutex's protocol (SYS_SYNC_PRIORITY, the default: the highest priority,
the oldest among equals; SYS_SYNC_FIFO: the oldest; SYS_SYNC_RETRY counts as
priority), _signal_all every waiter, _signal_to the named thread. Answers
follow RPCS3's sys_lwcond_.cpp, lv2/sys_lwcond.cpp and lv2/sys_sync.h
(schedule): waiting without the lwmutex is CELL_EPERM, expiry is
CELL_ETIMEDOUT with the lwmutex held, a signal with no waiter is lost
(CELL_OK), signal_to a thread that is not waiting is CELL_EPERM and to one
that does not exist CELL_ESRCH, destroying an lwcond a thread sleeps on is
CELL_EBUSY. sys_ppu_thread.c gains ppu_thread_query (exists? priority?).
The queue's mutex and condition variable are leaked heap objects, so a
guest thread still waiting while exit() runs static destructors never
waits on a destroyed object.

Why: the wait was a 1 ms poll and every signal a no-op, so every wait was a
spurious wakeup. Code whose wait has no predicate loop then runs on state
nobody published. Gran Turismo 6's event object (func_00A5F3DC: "if not
signalled, waiting = 1; lwcond_wait; waiting = 0") woke its file worker with
no request posted, the worker wrote through the NULL request, and boot died
in a stack overrun soon after.

One global queue lock and condition variable serve every lwcond: waking is
notify_all, and each waiter checks its own flag. Simple and enough for
boot; a per-lwcond channel would cut wakeups for titles with many waiters.

Test: tools/test_lwcond_wakeup.py (new: wakeups, timeouts, wake order per
protocol, EBUSY, ESRCH); tools/test_lwcond_layout.py now builds the waiter
queue too.

